WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.060
Broken access control is one of the most important risks in Node.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.127
js APIs and web applications.

00:00:07.127 --> 00:00:12.087
A route existing behind authentication does not automatically mean every object, action,

00:00:12.087 --> 00:00:13.740
or field is authorized.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.287
Object-level authorization asks whether this caller may access this specific record.

00:00:18.287 --> 00:00:22.420
Function-level authorization asks whether this caller may perform this action.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.553
Field-level decisions may also matter when APIs expose sensitive attributes.

00:00:26.553 --> 00:00:30.687
Injection risks appear when untrusted input influences commands, queries, templates,

00:00:30.687 --> 00:00:32.340
expressions, or downstream interpreters.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.713
The defensive pattern is to avoid string-built commands or queries, use safe APIs,

00:00:37.713 --> 00:00:41.020
validate inputs, encode outputs, and keep privileges limited.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.980
Server-rendered pages and API-backed applications also need to handle XSS risk by

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.460
controlling how untrusted content becomes HTML,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.113
JavaScript, URLs, or attributes.

00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:55.073
CSRF matters when browser cookies are used for authentication and requests can

00:00:55.073 --> 00:00:57.140
be triggered from another site.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.687
CORS is often misunderstood; it is a browser access control mechanism,

00:01:01.687 --> 00:01:04.580
not a replacement for authentication or authorization.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.713
A permissive CORS policy can create unintended browser-based access patterns,

00:01:08.713 --> 00:01:12.020
especially when combined with credentials or sensitive responses.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.393
Security headers, rate limiting, and careful response design all support defense in depth.

00:01:17.393 --> 00:01:22.353
APIs should avoid excessive data exposure by returning only what the caller

00:01:22.353 --> 00:01:24.833
needs and is authorized to see.

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:28.553
Most importantly, authorization must be enforced on the server.

00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:34.340
Hidden UI buttons, disabled form fields, or client-side route guards are useful for experience,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.820
but they are not security boundaries.
